{"title":"1976 IMO Problems/Problem 4","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Steakmath_(no_algebra;_basic)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Steakmath (no algebra; basic)</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Determine the greatest number, who is the product of some positive integers, and the sum of these numbers is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/e/4ce3274f66f14c069018efb0d8eecdb53bec64c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1976.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"39\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/1/6c1ff4802c7c3b13abd5ca6e3bf2c9ec82a12e8f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3*3=2*2*2+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"151\" height=\"14\" />, 3's are more efficient than 2's. We try to prove that 3's are more efficient than anything:\n</p><p>Let there be a positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is more efficient than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/a/d6a2f29516d8d85bf9120aba12b338bfff973969.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^3&lt;3^x$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"58\" height=\"15\" />. We try to prove that all integers greater than 3 are less efficient than 3:\n</p><p>When <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> increases by 1, then the RHS is multiplied by 3. The other side is multiplied by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/b/34ba23cfb863c554f587257c80a326a94aef449b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{(x+1)^3}{x^3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"65\" height=\"39\" />, and we must prove that this is less than 3 for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> greater than 3.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/8/a28105d6aec445088129be12ec2f3319904566aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{(x+1)^3}{x^3}&lt;3\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{x+1}{x}&lt;\\sqrt[3]{3}\\Rightarrow 1&lt;(\\sqrt[3]{3}-1)x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"368\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/a/5bab31a9e47443229acc477bb850b7f5b502d8a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{3}-1}&lt;x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"94\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p>Thus we need to prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/3/68342e162e8acb30679f59b8cda686c4f9cbd96d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{3}-1}&lt;4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"93\" height=\"41\" />. Simplifying, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/e/7de7f0440c51be58cdf7aac7498f7ca76114bb1a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5&lt;4\\sqrt[3]{3}\\Rightarrow 125&lt;64*3=192$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"242\" height=\"18\" />, which is true. Working backwards, we see that all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> greater than 3 are less efficient than 3, so we never use anything greater than 3. Therefore we use as many 3s in groups of 2 as possible, and since the remainder when 1976 is divided by 6 is 2, we can use 658 3s and one 2.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/0/a101b0ccc820911e90f7b3326beda20e7d74e0fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{1976}{3}=658.6666$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"135\" height=\"38\" />, so the greatest product is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/e/07e296c41ced4e8f6f131481cf767c509415d73d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{3^{658}\\cdot 2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"63\" height=\"26\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>(Non-rigorous)\nWe demonstrate heuristically that 3's are the most efficient. \nAs we know that the chosen numbers must be equal, by the AM-GM inequality, we wish to maximize the function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/8/e78e63c27898d26638e30a686a448468f6725cbe.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(x) = \\left(\\frac{1976}{x}\\right)^{x}\\]\" width=\"133\" height=\"43\" />\nSimple logarithmic differentiation shows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/1/761a302d62c23b2409261cca568aa020d89a9d5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{S}{x} = e$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"48\" height=\"37\" /> maximizes the given function. As <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/a/a3a59bb1293ee3f6dec19de4019a7178874219ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> is approximately 2.71828, we use 2's and 3's only. 3's are optimal, but we must use one 2.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><i>Note:  This solution uses the same strategy as Solution 1 (that having the largest possible number of three's is good), but approaches the proof in a different manner.</i>\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/6/2f6787e22de55f1970fdd538c3264df70ab1dc38.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(S) \\triangleq \\prod_{x \\in S} x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -21px\" width=\"99\" height=\"38\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/c/61c0fdc5382a8f7f20b2dd92eef71379d85d46e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g(S) = \\sum_{x\\in S} x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -21px\" width=\"98\" height=\"39\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is a multiset.  We fix <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/e/6ce97eb3353973791875122500721d0ca4d45c23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g(S) = 1976$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" />, and aim to maximize <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/c/86ca6c1c34ee45eabb5363a352de12e1455f8285.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(S)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" />.  Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/e/46e128dc918b7b66fc98898b04608340b53938c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3(x-3) &gt; x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"99\" height=\"18\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/b/44b46f646617709f9f25b41bb5014adb2884a461.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\geq 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" />,  we notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> must only contain the  integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/c/f3c13f88915d101b401e1fdeac1b195262655caa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,2,3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.  We can replace any occurrences of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> by replacing it with a couple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s, without changing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/c/86ca6c1c34ee45eabb5363a352de12e1455f8285.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(S)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/1/771dc3e33c06cb291b3eeb0bedc1de181c11a480.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g(S)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" />, so we may assume that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> only contains the integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/3/2d338f393d100febd19e4284537ac40517b48340.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"28\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.   We may further assume that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> contains at most one <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />,  since any two <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s can be replaced by a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> without changing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/1/771dc3e33c06cb291b3eeb0bedc1de181c11a480.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g(S)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" />, but with an increase in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/c/86ca6c1c34ee45eabb5363a352de12e1455f8285.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(S)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" />.  If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> contains exactly one <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, then it must also contain at least one <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> (since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/5/2d5fa0133ac9928aa5a3014a9533b91f9dcfd3c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1976 \\equiv 2\\;(\\mathrm{mod }\\;3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"136\" height=\"18\" />).   We can then replace this pair of a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> with a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, thus keeping <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/1/771dc3e33c06cb291b3eeb0bedc1de181c11a480.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g(S)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" /> constant, and increasing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/c/86ca6c1c34ee45eabb5363a352de12e1455f8285.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(S)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" />.   Now we may assume that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> contains only <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s.   \n</p><p>Now, as observed in the last solution, any triplet of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s can be replaced by a couple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s, with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/1/771dc3e33c06cb291b3eeb0bedc1de181c11a480.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g(S)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" /> constant, and an increase in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/c/86ca6c1c34ee45eabb5363a352de12e1455f8285.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(S)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" />.    Thus, after repeating this operation, we will be left with at most two <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s.  Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/e/6ce97eb3353973791875122500721d0ca4d45c23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g(S) = 1976$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/5/2d5fa0133ac9928aa5a3014a9533b91f9dcfd3c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1976 \\equiv 2\\;(\\mathrm{mod }\\;3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"136\" height=\"18\" />,  we therefore get that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> must have exactly one <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> (since we already showed it consists only of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s).   Thus, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/a/21ab34ac4abbf1260ba70d721324ac38a668530a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{f(S) = 2\\cdot 3^{658}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"125\" height=\"31\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>We determine the greatest number, who is the product of some positive integers, and the sum of these numbers is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/e/4ce3274f66f14c069018efb0d8eecdb53bec64c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1976.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"39\" height=\"13\" />. \n</p><p>For this problem, we used induction where if we assumed that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/1/3b1be2340079411dac99664d3857725babacc96e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i, j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"22\" height=\"16\" /> are greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Then \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/b/e3b648126bd357a031573b2e65aeeb9cdaeca74a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2^i \\cdot 3^j &gt; k\\]\" width=\"77\" height=\"16\" />\nwhere <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/f/23f19939eaa5742378faf65641037a3754f6a653.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = i \\cdot 2 + j \\cdot 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"118\" height=\"18\" />. Knowing this implies that the optimal solution comprises of twos and threes as every integer greater than 1 can be expressed as a linear combination of twos and threes, we proceeded to show that given a configuration of \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/d/b4d296e87948eac1d09dd9eb8fd98315e4c452d5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2^i \\cdot 3^k,\\]\" width=\"48\" height=\"19\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/4/27422da08e3f81ee70ace9bb64d354291e5d4e1f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2^{i+n} \\cdot 3^{\\,j-\\tfrac{2}{3}n}\\]\" width=\"92\" height=\"23\" /> \nis less than the original expression, implying that maximal multiplication by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is maximizing under the given relationship with powers of two.\n</p><p>More rigorously, we can show that maximizing with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is optimal by comparing the expression \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/4/1b43beb24fb17960c61eaa478ab554d406d1151d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2^i \\cdot 3^j\\]\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" />\nwith \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/1/6410b7d7057f2cf28f1816e93b98410787518928.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2^{i+n} \\cdot 3^{\\,j-\\tfrac{2}{3}n}.\\]\" width=\"97\" height=\"23\" />\nTaking the ratio gives\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/1/de1eb432ebdf6266529a8d659856ff552e0599dd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{2^{i+n} \\cdot 3^{\\,j-\\tfrac{2}{3}n}}{2^i \\cdot 3^j} = \\frac{2^n}{3^{\\tfrac{2}{3}n}}.\\]\" width=\"156\" height=\"56\" />\nNow consider the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-th root of this factor:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/a/87a25649d7fb89f81cc949e5558e2f7193adab57.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\left(\\frac{2^n}{3^{\\tfrac{2}{3}n}}\\right)^{\\!\\!1/n} = \\frac{2}{3^{2/3}} &lt; 1.\\]\" width=\"169\" height=\"58\" />\nThus multiplying by more <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />’s (rather than shifting weight into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />’s) always gives a larger product, confirming that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is maximizing under the given relationship. \n</p><p>However, when we reach an edge, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/d/00d8ec46cc4acc7d15403c12ded759eed182f962.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\cdot 2$\" width=\"30\" height=\"12\" /> over <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/d/b7d8d1f7bf72c281f742fc014d77a3e140756ef9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\cdot 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" /> is a superior solution. Given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/4/b146679dc9b3c5ecc337c4b90450a40e0283f6ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1976$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" /> we can have \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/b/93bf8e910c2ab2fbae0235999344f07747c33274.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3^{658} \\cdot 2.\\]\" width=\"55\" height=\"15\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>This is a simple problem, but in some of the other solutions it wasn't totally clear to me what was going on. \n</p><p>Let some product satisfying the conditions be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />. Suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> contains a factor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/c/5ac3f285dab842d47881054119dc7bfd7542d60b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p \\geq 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"44\" height=\"17\" />. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/f/46f94cbc33b754cd697f50d84f33848b33558da6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M \\leq \\frac{M}{p}(p-2)2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"133\" height=\"43\" />. So we may assume WLOG all terms are less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Clearly the product can be increased if it contains <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Similarly if the the product contains three <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s then it can be increased by replacing the three <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s with two <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s. So an optimal product (which we showed only contains terms less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> WLOG) contains zero <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s, and zero, one, or two <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s. The only product satisfying these conditions is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/6/1269f563609efde68fbd11b34952463c9369a865.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3^{658} \\cdot 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"17\" />. \n</p><p>~not_detriti\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_Steakmath_.28no_algebra.3B_basic.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Steakmath_(no_algebra;_basic)\">Video Solution by Steakmath (no algebra; basic)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Steakmath (no algebra; basic)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/jXThxHw6RM8\">https://youtu.be/jXThxHw6RM8</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO\" title=\"1976 IMO\">1976 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1976 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1976 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1976 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1976 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1976 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1976 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1976 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1976_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1976 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">Problem 5</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017172245\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.045 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.156 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 555/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 894/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 44/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 18022/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   17.482      1 Template:IMO_box\n100.00%   17.482      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:6415:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017172245 and revision id 285071. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}